WORLD // NEXUS — Comprehensive System Architecture & Implementation Specification


Core Philosophy: WORLD // NEXUS does not render data. It renders states of knowledge about the world.


I. System Overview & Governing Equation


WORLD // NEXUS is a planetary-scale spatiotemporal knowledge engine designed to represent civilization through a unified model of entities, relationships, space, time, provenance, and epistemic certainty.


The engine obeys the formal state-space equation:

W = F(E, R, S, T, P, Q, \Gamma)

Where:




E (Entities): Distinct real-world or digital objects (companies, assets, people, nodes, vessels, aircraft).


R (Relationships): Directed, typed edges linking entities (e.g., operates, owns, routes_to).


S (Spatial State): Geographic coordinates, footprints, and H3-indexed spatial distributions.


T (Transactions / Bitemporal Model): Dual tracking of Valid Time (when a fact was true in reality) and Observation Time (when the system recorded it).


P (Provenance): Append-only, verifiable chains tracing every assertion back to original sources and records.


Q (Query Context): Dynamic user parameters, including viewport zoom, spatial radius, active filters, and temporal focus.


\Gamma (Epistemic State): Multi-factor confidence vectors and explicit epistemic classification boundaries.




II. Architectural Constitution & Core Rules




The Cardinal Rule (Renderer Independence): The rendering engine is never the source of truth. It is a pure projection of the World-State Engine. The same underlying state can be projected into WebGL (3D globe), REST APIs, analytical terminals, or automated reports without mutating state.


Distributed Engine over Unified State: At scale, the World-State Engine abstracts distributed stores, event streams, and graph indexes, presenting a single coherent state interface.


Minimum-Representation Principle: The system scales via spatial aggregation (H3 hexagonal grids), volumetric textures, and Level-of-Detail (LOD) compression rather than rendering raw, unaggregated entity clouds at global scale.




III. Epistemic Classification & Confidence Vectors


1. Epistemic States


The system strictly enforces six discrete epistemic classifications without silent data transformation:




KNOWN: Directly verified via authoritative registries or cryptographic proof.


INFERRED: Strongly supported structural relationships derived via multi-source correlation.


ESTIMATED: Model-derived approximations (statistical, economic, or spatial simulations).


CONFLICTED: Unresolved contradictions between trusted sources (preserved explicitly rather than forced).


UNKNOWN: Explicit absence of reliable data (unknown \neq zero).


RESTRICTED: Information exists within structural or legal systems but lacks legitimate public accessibility.




2. Multi-Factor Confidence Vector (C)


Confidence is decomposed into seven independent dimensions to prevent nuance collapse:




Source Reliability: Credibility history of the reporting authority.


Measurement Quality: Precision and fidelity of the initial capture.


Temporal Freshness: Proximity of the observation to current valid time.


Cross-Source Agreement: Verification across independent registries/channels.


Methodological Confidence: Rigor of the inference or ingestion method.


Spatial Accuracy: Precision of geographic attribution.


Completeness: Absence of missing data fields in the record.




IV. Bitemporal Data Architecture


Every entity and relationship maintains an immutable bitemporal ledger:




Valid Time (valid_from → valid_to): The interval during which the state was true in the physical or digital world.


Observation Time / Transaction Time (observed_at): The exact timestamp when NEXUS ingested or registered the observation.


Capability: Enables retrospective temporal slicing ("What did NEXUS believe on date X regarding valid state on date Y?") and tracks confidence evolution across revisions (Version 1 \to Version 2 \to Version 3).




V. Semantic Resolution (Zoom Levels)


Navigation shifts the fundamental meaning and granularity of the visualization across six distinct tiers:




Zoom 1 — Earth (Planetary): Planetary hexagonal population density, economic fields, global network corridors, and aggregate flow vectors.


Zoom 2 — Continent (Regional): National boundaries, continental infrastructure, Tier-1 autonomous systems, and major transport corridors.


Zoom 3 — Country (National): Regional clusters, enterprise footprints, national facilities, and major transport hubs.


Zoom 4 — City (Urban): Urban density fields, local infrastructure networks, and metropolitan asset clustering.


Zoom 5 — Entity (Asset/Organization): Individual companies, aircraft, vessels, domains, ASNs, and interactive relationship graphs.


Zoom 6 — Record (Provenance): Raw source logs, registry certificates, cryptographic proofs, and complete observation history chains.




VI. User Interface & Inspector Requirements


1. The 3D Planetary Observatory View




Physically plausible Earth rendering with atmospheric scattering, night-side city illumination, and real-time volumetric data layers.


Dynamic toggles for system layers: Population, Economy, Internet Topology, Air Traffic, Maritime Traffic, Financial Markets, Blockchain, Companies, Infrastructure, Environment.




2. The Knowledge Inspector




A contextual side panel displaying target entities, confidence radar vectors, live relationship edges, and complete provenance evidence trails.


Interactive resolution tracing from screen pixels back through aggregation grids, entities, events, provenance chains, and source documents.




VII. Phase 0 Vertical Slice Implementation Scope


To build the initial prototype, developers must scope the environment and data ingestion pipeline as follows:




Geographic Boundary: San Francisco sector.


Entity Count: ~100 core entities spanning aviation (flights/aircraft), maritime (vessels), corporate registries, domain/ASN infrastructure, blockchain txns, and physical properties.


Mandatory Interactive Validation Loop (Definition of Done):


Initialize the system and render the 3D globe.


Focus viewport on San Francisco.


Zoom in to transition from planetary aggregation to local knowledge fields.


Select a specific entity to trigger the Knowledge Inspector.


Inspect its bitemporal state, multi-factor confidence, and provenance chain.


Modify the temporal navigation slider and observe state evolution over time.


Adjust epistemic/confidence filters and observe dynamic projection changes.


Zoom out to verify the entity gracefully dissolves back into aggregate spatial density fields.







